<%#
  The project folder's ROOT, shown as the Files panel (stage 3, S3-1; folder panels 2026-09-08):
  loose root files first (the sweep's inbox), then Generated (tool output, once, never repeated in
  the folder it sits in), Backups, and every attachment that points OUTSIDE the folder ("Linked
  from elsewhere"). Each real SUBFOLDER is its own panel now — see the note below.
  Maker::ProjectFolderListing (@listing) computes the tree, the loose files and the cross-cutting
  Backups list; ProjectsController#show computes Generated (Maker::AddonOrigin.for) and Linked
  (diffing @attachments against @listing's own tree).

  THE FOLDER AND THE RELATION ARE TWO DIFFERENT THINGS, AND THE PANEL SHOWS BOTH. `@attachments` is
  the file→project RELATION ProjectsController#show has always computed (detach, cover, addon
  origin, the missing count) — but a file dropped straight into the project's folder on the NAS is
  listed here even though nothing ever attached it: the folder IS the relation for a plain folder
  row, whose `attachment` is nil. A file that IS attached but still lives somewhere else in the
  library renders once, in Linked, with Detach and a button to move it in. `@page_nodes` (PageNodes)
  is what the Linked rows below (and the build-log photos elsewhere on this page) read to find
  their node; a folder row needs no such lookup — its node comes straight off the listing's own walk.

  `@missing_attachment_count` — attached rows whose node ProjectsController#show could not find
  live at all — is unrelated to either list above and is reported on its own, below.

  `@page_thumbs` (ThumbnailMap) is best-effort and INDEPENDENT OF LIVENESS, which is the distinction
  to keep: a backend blip degrades a tile to its format badge, a different condition from a file
  being gone. It is exactly why liveness reads `nodes.present` and not the port, for the folder
  listing as much as for the Linked rows — ThumbnailMap rescues FetchError to {}, so an unmounted
  NAS would otherwise empty this panel and report every file deleted. Pinned by page_nodes_test.rb.

  ONE `thumbs` controller wraps every group in THIS panel rather than one per grid: Stimulus finds
  targets anywhere in the controller element's subtree, so a single instance polls all pending
  cells beneath it instead of N instances polling their own slice. `filemenu` rides along on the
  same element for the same reason — one document-level listener, every tile. A folder panel is a
  SIBLING subtree and carries its own pair; there is no element that could cover both.

  THE PANEL OPENS WITH THE FOLDER'S OWN CARD (stage 4, S4-1): `projects/_repo_card` over
  ProjectsController#show's @root_card when @listing.root_repo? (the folder IS a working tree —
  the head also carries a `repo` badge and Commits/History, and the loose caption says the sweep
  leaves those files alone), else `projects/_readme_card` over @readme when the folder has a plain
  README. Neither is inside the "no files yet" check below: a README is content.

  THE PANEL IS THE FOLDER'S ROOT NOW, NOT THE WHOLE TREE (2026-09-08, folder panels; spec
  decision 4). Every DEPTH-ONE subfolder is its own panel (projects/_folder_panel), so the
  `file_tree_section` collection render that used to sit between Loose and Generated is gone from
  here — deleting it is what stops a folder rendering twice. What stays is everything that is NOT
  a subfolder: the root's card, Loose, and the cross-cutting Generated, Backups and Linked groups,
  plus the truncation and missing-attachment notes.

  `view` IS THIS PANEL'S OWN, read once here off @panel_layout and passed DOWN as a required local
  — the page-wide `project_view` cookie retired with the toggle that set it (spec decision 6), so
  there is no ivar for a group partial to read any more. `browser` is not offered: there is no Dir
  for the folder's own root to hand projects/_repo_browser unless the folder IS a working tree, and
  a control that does nothing is worse than one that is absent. A `browser` value stored by hand
  renders as tiles, which is what every group already does with anything that is not "list".
%>
<% view = @panel_layout.view_for("attachments") %>
<%# `id="attachments"`: the view toggle below redirects to this panel's anchor, and a dead anchor
    is worse than no panel (Maker::ProjectKind's own argument for ALWAYS; found in review). %>
<section class="panel attachments-panel" id="attachments">
  <div class="panel-head">
    <h2><%= panel_title(@panel_layout, "attachments") %></h2>
    <%# The folder's own path, so the panel says WHERE it is showing, not just what. Nothing to
        link to when the folder ensure failed this render (an unmounted NAS, a bucket blip) — the
        upload control below already degrades the same way for the same reason. %>
    <%= link_to @listing.path, files_path(folder: @listing.folder_id), class: "panel-path mono muted" if @listing.available? %>
    <% if @listing.root_repo? %><span class="badge">repo</span><% end %>
    <div class="panel-actions">
      <%# THE ROOT'S OWN Commits/History (stage 4, S4-1). A nested repository carries these on its
          section summary and inside its card; a project whose FOLDER is the working tree has no
          section of its own to hang them on, so the panel head is where they belong. %>
      <% if @listing.root_repo? %>
        <%= link_to "Commits", repo_commit_path(@listing.folder_id), class: "linkbtn" %>
        <%= link_to "History", repo_history_path(@listing.folder_id), class: "linkbtn" %>
      <% end %>
      <%# Tiles / List, FOR THIS PANEL (2026-09-08, folder panels). The same view-toggle component
          the Files page uses (components.css), but the choice is a WRITE now — it persists on the
          project row under this panel's key — so these are button_to forms, not links, and the
          door redirects back to this panel's anchor. projects/_folder_panel.html.erb carries the
          same markup over all three of Maker::Project::VIEWS; see its header for why Files omits
          `browser`. %>
      <div class="view-toggle" role="group" aria-label="View">
        <% Maker::ProjectKind.offered_views("attachments").each do |value| %>
          <%= button_to value.capitalize, project_panels_path(@project), method: :patch,
                        params: { view: { "attachments" => value } },
                        class: "toggle#{' active' if view == value}" %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
      <%# "Look at my library NOW", instead of waiting on library_scan's 15 minutes (share/s3) or
          remote_changelog_pull's 2 (chunk_remote). It scans the LIBRARY, not this folder — a scan
          cannot be aimed at a subfolder (Library::Reconciler compares against the whole catalog), so
          the door refreshes the store and the existing chains do the per-project filing. %>
      <%= button_to "Scan folder", project_scan_path(@project), method: :post, class: "linkbtn" %>
      <%= link_to "Attach existing", new_project_attachment_path(@project), class: "linkbtn" %>
      <%= render "upload_control", upload_url: project_uploads_path(@project),
                 attach_url: project_attachments_path(@project), redirect_url: project_path(@project),
                 parent_node_id: @upload_parent_node_id %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <%= panel_intro(@panel_layout, "attachments") %>

  <%#
    THE FOLDER IS IN THE TRASH, AND THE PAGE SAYS SO (stage 4, S4-6; decision 2). Deleting a
    project's folder in Finder or from the Files page is the maker's own act, so
    Maker::Project#ensure_library_folder! no longer mints a replacement behind their back — it
    answers nil, this panel loses its listing, and the maker is told which of the two things
    happened and what to do about either. Without the sentence the panel is simply empty, which
    reads as "this project has no files" — a lie about a folder full of them sitting in the trash.

    `folder_node_id.present?` IS WHAT SPLITS THE TWO CASES: a project that never had a folder (the
    ensure failed at create time, an unmounted NAS) has nothing in the trash to restore, and the
    empty state below already speaks for it.
  %>
  <% if @project.folder_node_id.present? && !@listing.available? %>
    <p class="muted folder-missing">
      This project's folder is in the trash or was removed.
      <%= link_to "Restore it", trash_index_path %>, or make another folder a project.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# WHAT THE SWEEP DID, ON THE PAGE (stage 4, S4-2; story 1.5). Both branches are facts the maker
      needs and neither is an error: a project that files itself says when it last did, and one
      that has been told not to says so where the files are, not only on the edit form. %>
  <% if @project.autofile? %>
    <% if @project.last_sweep_moves.to_i.positive? && @project.last_swept_at.present? %>
      <p class="muted sweep-note">
        Sweep filed <%= pluralize(@project.last_sweep_moves, "file") %>
        · <%= time_ago_in_words(@project.last_swept_at) %> ago
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <p class="muted sweep-note">
      Automatic filing is off for this project.
      <%= link_to "Turn it on", edit_project_path(@project) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%# A share/s3 scan is a BACKGROUND job — an unbounded NAS walk or bucket listing — so the page
      that started it cannot show the result. The element POLLS for a real "scan finished" (stage 4,
      S4-3) and then reloads once, to the param-free url, which is what stops it looping: the
      reloaded page carries no ?scanning=1, renders no element, and polls nothing. `since` is the
      stamp THIS render read, so the poll can tell "a scan finished after this page was built" from
      "the last one finished before it". Rendered only on the async path: chunk_remote finished
      inside the request. `class="muted"` because the give-up sentence lands in this element.
      §14d — a data-controller name that resolves to nothing is a SILENT no-op, which is why
      app/javascript/controllers/scanreload_controller.js must exist and stimulus_hygiene_test
      guards that it does. %>
  <% if @scan_started && capabilities.scannable? %>
    <span class="muted" data-controller="scanreload"
          data-scanreload-url-value="<%= project_path(@project) %>"
          data-scanreload-status-url-value="<%= scan_status_files_path %>"
          data-scanreload-since-value="<%= current_user.library_scanned_stamp %>"></span>
  <% end %>

  <%# THE ROOT'S CARD (stage 4, S4-1), above the groups, for every project that has one: the
      repository card when the folder itself is a working tree (README, latest commit, tag), the
      README alone otherwise. Outside the empty check below on purpose — a README is content.
      STAGE 4, S4-4: the card renders even with NO README, because it now carries the "Write a
      README" door (@readme_editable is true whenever the folder is available). %>
  <% if @root_card %>
    <%= render "projects/repo_card", card: @root_card, project: @project,
               readme_row: @readme_row, editable: @readme_editable %>
  <% elsif @readme || @readme_editable %>
    <%= render "projects/readme_card", readme: @readme, editable: @readme_editable,
               readme_row: @readme_row, project: @project %>
  <% end %>

  <%# BACKUPS COME OUT FIRST, pulled out of the tree wherever they physically sit and keyed on the
      NODE's name rather than any attachment's label — a folder row carries no attachment at all,
      and Row#backup? always reads the node. They keep their own closed section rather than being
      dropped: a rescue copy is still a real file in the project's folder, and a panel that
      pretended otherwise would be lying about what is on disk. Filtered against @generated_ids
      like every other group, for the rare case an
      addon's own output happens to look like one. %>
  <% backups = @listing.backups.reject { |r| @generated_ids.include?(r.node_id) } %>
  <% loose = @listing.loose.reject { |r| @generated_ids.include?(r.node_id) } %>
  <% if @listing.tree.empty? && loose.empty? && backups.empty? && @linked_rows.empty? &&
        @generated.empty? && @missing_attachment_count.zero? && !@listing.truncated? %>
    <%# THE TREE STAYS IN THIS TEST THOUGH ITS SECTIONS LEFT: the sentence is about the PROJECT,
        not about this panel, and a project whose folder holds nothing but subfolders has files —
        they are in the panels below. Saying "no files in this project yet" over three full folder
        panels would be the flat lie the folder-missing note above exists to avoid. %>
    <p class="muted">No files in this project yet.<% if @listing.available? %> Upload one, or drop files into <span class="mono"><%= @listing.path %></span>.<% end %></p>
  <% else %>
    <%# THE WRAPPER IS CONDITIONAL because this panel's groups are, all four of them: with the
        subfolder sections gone, a folder holding only subfolders leaves nothing here to poll or
        right-click, and an empty `thumbs` div would be a controller instance over no cells. %>
    <% if loose.any? || @generated.any? || backups.any? || @linked_rows.any? %>
      <div data-controller="thumbs filemenu" data-thumbs-url-value="<%= thumbnails_files_path %>">
        <%# LOOSE FIRST — the sweep's inbox. Shown only when there is something in it; the note says
            why these files sit apart from every real section instead of reading as forgotten. %>
        <% if loose.any? %>
          <%= render "attachment_group", key: "loose", label: "Loose", rows: loose, open: true, view: %>
          <%# THE CAPTION MUST BE TRUE OF THIS FOLDER (stage 4, S4-1): the sweep does not file a
              working tree's files, so promising it would be a lie about the one shape most real
              project folders have. %>
          <% if @listing.root_repo? %>
            <p class="muted loose-note">This folder is a git repository, so its files stay where they are.</p>
          <% else %>
            <p class="muted loose-note">Loose files are filed on the next sweep; a README stays here.</p>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>

        <%# TOOL OUTPUT, once, never repeated inside whichever section it sits in. %>
        <% if @generated.any? %>
          <%= render "generated_group", view: %>
        <% end %>

        <%# COLLAPSED, and that is the whole of "don't show the backups": they are one click away
            rather than gone, so nothing about the project's folder is hidden from someone looking
            for it. %>
        <% if backups.any? %>
          <%= render "attachment_group", key: "backups", label: "Backups", rows: backups, open: false, view: %>
        <% end %>

        <%# ATTACHED, BUT LIVING ELSEWHERE. Not a folder row and not counted in @listing.total — its
            own section, always open, so it reads as "these still need your attention" rather than
            being buried among the folder's own files. %>
        <% if @linked_rows.any? %>
          <%= render "attachment_group", key: "linked", label: "Linked from elsewhere", rows: @linked_rows, open: true, view: %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <% if @missing_attachment_count.positive? %>
      <p class="muted attachments-missing">
        <%= pluralize(@missing_attachment_count, "attached file") %> no longer in your library.
        <%= link_to "Check the trash", trash_index_path %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# OUTSIDE the empty check on purpose: a walk that hit DIR_CAP before reaching a single file
      would otherwise say "No files in this project yet" and nothing else (found in review). Two
      sentences for two caps — `total` counts only the dirs that were walked, so "0 of 0" is not
      what a cut-short walk means. %>
  <% if @listing.truncated? %>
    <p class="muted attachments-truncated">
      <% if @listing.all_rows.size < @listing.total %>Showing <%= @listing.all_rows.size %> of <%= @listing.total %> files.<% end %>
      <% if @listing.dir_cap_hit? %>Not every subfolder was listed.<% end %>
      <%= link_to "Open the folder", files_path(folder: @listing.folder_id) %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</section>
